These six lines are the **complete** set of pass criteria the spike's own G1
gate code evaluated against during the rerun. The criteria are hard-coded in
`Experiments/TranscriptSpike/Sources/.../GateG1.swift` and emitted via
`logger.log("G1 criterion ...")` at the very start of the gate's `run()` function,
ahead of any measurement.

## Decoded pass criteria (for human reading)

| Line | Field | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| L372 | `soak_seconds` | `1800` | 30-minute target soak window |
| L373 | `web_content_count` | `1` | Exactly one spike-attributable WebContent process |
| L374 | `rss_total_max_bytes` | `419430400` | App + WebContent RSS ≤ 400 MB (combined budget) |
| L375 | `sample_interval_sec` | `60` | 60-second sampling cadence |
| L376 | `plateau_window_seconds` | `600` | Final 10 min = plateau window |
| L376 | `tolerance_mb` | `20` | Plateau spread ≤ 20 MB |
| L377 | `message_count_source` | `GRDB` | Message count comes from local GRDB, not the wire |

## Why this is the authoritative pre-registration

- It is **timestamped** (`2026-08-05T13:22:31.933Z`) and **dated** to the second.
- It was **printed by the spike's own code** — not retroactively written by the
  operator or the attribution tool — so it cannot have been edited after the
  fact to match the measured numbers.
- It was **emitted before the run** (the first attribution sample fired 109 ms
  later at `13:22:32.042Z`; the verdict fired 21 min later at `13:44:32.301Z`).
- The numbers here match the WP-0 protocol spec verbatim (1800 s soak, 1
  process, 400 MB total, 600 s / 20 MB plateau) — confirming the spike was
  configured against the spec, not retroactively adjusted.

## Companion artefact: the attribution-tool overlay

The attribution-tool (`attribut-webcontent.sh`) checked the run against a
**stricter per-spike overlay** (spike_count ∈ {1,2}, per-spike RSS ≤ 200 MB,
spike PID stable, idle residual ≤ 10, Safari/mail/messages/notes = 0). Those
overlay criteria live in `attribut-webcontent.sh` and are NOT part of this
pre-registration block — they are a corroborating second pass applied after
the spike's own G1 gate finished. See `G1-evidence-rerun.md` §
"Pre-registered criteria (timestamped, spike-emitted) vs attribution overlay"
for the reconciliation between the two sets and the rationale for the
number difference (200 MB per-spike vs 400 MB combined).